08_structure.md
A struct is used to store multiple related pieces of data under one name. 
Unlike arrays, which store only one type of data, a struct can store different data types together,
such as integers, strings, and booleans. It helps organize data in a cleaner way,
<br>
2. Key C# Syntax
<br>
struct Student
{
    public string name;
    public int age;
}

Student s1;
s1.name = "Alex";
s1.age = 20;

Console.WriteLine(s1.name);
<br>
3. Eureka Exercise / Moment
<br>
At first, I didn’t understand why we needed structs when we already had variables. I was confused about how they were different from arrays.
What made it click was realizing that structs can store different types of data together
<br>
4. Common Beginner Mistake
<br>
A common mistake is forgetting to create an instance of the struct before using it. 
This happens because beginners think defining the struct automatically creates usable variables, but you must create an object first.
